[saleor/graphql/payment/mutations/transaction/transaction_request_action.py] IMHO, I would suggest to raise an error for each field present in input, like:
```python
        if reason_exists and action_type != TransactionAction.REFUND:
            errors = {}
            if reason:
                errors["reason"] = ValidationError(
                    "`reason` can be set only for REFUND action.",
                    code=TransactionRequestActionErrorCode.INVALID.value,
                )
            if reason_reference_id:
                errors["reason_reference"] = ValidationError(
                    "`reasonReference` can be set only for REFUND action.",
                    code=TransactionRequestActionErrorCode.INVALID.value,
                )
            raise ValidationError(errors)
```
The field used as a `key` (in my example: `reason` and `reason_reference`) represents the current field name from the input. So user will receive better feedback, as the raised errors will be related to the fields that was used incorrectly.

[saleor/graphql/order/mutations/order_grant_refund_create.py] It should be split into two separate `try except blocks`.
`from_global_id_or_error` is raising `GraphQLError` so you should catch it and convert into `ValidationError` to make it clear to the user which field is invalid. On `BaseMutation` there is already function that is doing that `get_global_id_or_error`, so you can use it instead.
```suggestion
            cls.get_global_id_or_error(reason_reference_id, "Page", field="reason_reference")
            try:
                Page.objects.get(
                        pk=reason_reference_pk, page_type=refund_reason_reference_type
                    )
             except Page.DoesNotExist as error:
                 raise ValidationError(
                    {
                        "reason_reference": ValidationError(
                            "Invalid reason reference, do not match the reference reason type.",
                            code=OrderGrantRefundCreateErrorCode.INVALID.value,
                        )
                    }
                ) from error
```

[saleor/core/notification/validation.py] If you look at other error codes in Saleor, all of them have `REQUIRED` error code, that can be used when an input field is not provided. In `ValidationError` we can also provide the name of the specific input field that is causing this error. Instead of adding a custom error code, we should add the `REQUIRED` error code and rewrite this to:

```suggestion
    raise ValidationError(
        {
            "ids": ValidationError(
                "The obligatory param 'ids' is missing or is empty.",
                code=ExternalNotificationTriggerErrorCode.REQUIRED,
            )
        }
    )
```
Please adjust the below function as well accordingly.

